WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.130
What is search worth? Sizing an SEO opportunity honestly.

2
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.720
This is the video for module one of the professional SEO course.

3
00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:14.150
At beginner level, you learn that SEO helps a business show up on Google.

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:22.370
At professional level, a client asks something harder. How much is better search visibility worth to us, and where should we start?

5
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:29.280
In this video you will answer that with the website's own numbers, not borrowed industry figures.

6
00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:35.030
You will also learn the key technical words, and the three markets you can work in.

7
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:43.360
Every search visit passes through the same chain. First, Google shows a page in results. That is an impression.

8
00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:54.960
Some searchers click. Clicks divided by impressions is the click-through rate, or CTR. You find these numbers in Search Console, in the Performance report.

9
00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:02.950
Some visitors take a valuable action, like an order, a booking or a quote request. That is a conversion.

10
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:10.390
Finally, each conversion is worth some amount. The owner tells you the average order or job value.

11
00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:24.910
Step one. Remove branded queries, the ones with the business name. People searching those already know the business. Non-branded queries describe a need, and that is where SEO wins new customers.

12
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:38.000
Step two. Find striking-distance queries, at an average position of about 8 to 20. Google already sees the page as relevant, so improving it is usually the faster win.

13
00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:48.910
Step three. Do not use CTR charts from the internet. Look at the client's own queries in positions 3 to 5 and use their average CTR.

14
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:57.160
Step four. Build two cases. An optimistic one with that CTR, and a conservative one with half of it.

15
00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:09.030
Google's own guidance says no one can guarantee a number one ranking. So label your estimate as a scenario, show the inputs and the formula, and give both cases.

16
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:20.670
Kemi's query, ankara fabric wholesale lagos, had 12,000 impressions, 96 clicks and an average position of 11.4 over three months.

17
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:29.980
Her queries in positions 3 to 5 average a 6% CTR, and 1.5% of her organic visitors place an order.

18
00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:35.630
Her average order is about 45,000 naira, from her own records.

19
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:50.490
In the conservative case, a 3% CTR gives 360 clicks, so 264 extra clicks. That is about 178,200 naira of extra sales.

20
00:02:51.740 --> 00:03:04.920
In the optimistic case, 6% gives 720 clicks, so 624 extra clicks and about 9 extra orders. That is about 421,200 naira.

21
00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:11.390
So this one query could be worth a range, not a guarantee, and the page deserves attention first.

22
00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:20.980
Developers judge you by how precisely you speak. Every server answers with a three-digit status code. 200 means OK.

23
00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:28.360
301 and 308 are permanent. Google treats the new address as the main one.

24
00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:36.260
302 and 307 are temporary. Google follows them but may keep the old address.

25
00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:48.220
404 means not found. 5xx means a server error. And point every old address straight to the final one in one hop, with no redirect chains.

26
00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:56.960
A Disallow line in robots.txt stops Google fetching a page, but the page can still be indexed from links.

27
00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:08.040
A noindex tag keeps a page out of results, but Google must crawl the page to see it. So if you want a page out of search, use noindex and let Google crawl it.

28
00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:20.220
You can work in three markets. Local businesses need local search, simple sites and fast pages on modest phones, because most visitors pay for data by the bundle.

29
00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:35.500
Multi-country brands need hreflang, which tells Google which version is for which country. Use valid codes like en-NG and en-GB, never en-UK, and every version must list itself and all the others.

30
00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:46.030
Remote international clients need technical audits at scale and clear written reports. Many people start local, then add remote work later.

31
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:56.590
To remember. Search value flows through impressions, CTR, conversions and value per conversion. Focus on non-branded queries.

32
00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:06.920
Striking-distance queries are often the fastest wins. Use the client's own numbers and give a range. And never mix up robots.txt and noindex.

33
00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:20.500
Now try it on a site you can access. Export the non-branded queries, list five in striking distance, work out the site's own CTR, and build both estimates with your formulas visible.

34
00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.790
Continue with the lessons below.
